= A
{parent=2004}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Groups in Action}

= A2
{parent=2004/A}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2004/A/A2}

The <permutation> inverse is $\rho_{g^{-1}}$: applying the two maps successively sends $\omega$ to $(\omega g)g^{-1}=\omega$. With the paper's right-action convention, <permutation> products also act on the right, and
$$
\omega(\rho_g\rho_h)=(\omega g)h=\omega(gh)=\omega\rho_{gh}.
$$
Thus $\boxed{\rho(gh)=\rho(g)\rho(h)}$. If function composition is written on the left instead, the same right action is represented by $g\mapsto\rho_{g^{-1}}$; keeping these conventions consistent avoids an artificial reversal. The kernel consists precisely of elements fixing every point, so
$$
\boxed{\ker\rho=\bigcap_{\omega\in\Omega}G_\omega.}
$$

The rotational tetrahedral <group> acts transitively on its four vertices. A <rotation> fixing a vertex must preserve its opposite face and rotate its three vertices cyclically; its <stabilizer> has order three. The <orbit-stabilizer theorem> gives $|G|=4\cdot3=12$. The rotations are the identity, two nontrivial rotations about each of the four vertex-to-opposite-face axes, and a half-turn about each of the three opposite-edge-midpoint axes. Therefore the counts by element order are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|rrrrrr}n&1&2&3&4&6&12\\\#&1&3&8&0&0&0\end{array}.}
$$
A <rotation> fixing all vertices fixes three <linearly independent> vertex vectors and is the identity. The vertex <permutation> action is therefore faithful. The listed rotations induce the identity, 3-cycles and double transpositions, all even permutations. Its injective image has order 12, the full order of $A_4$. Hence $\boxed{G\cong A_4}$.

= B
{parent=2004}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Introduction to Fields}

= B2
{parent=2004/B}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2004/B/B2}

Take $\boxed{K=F[x]/(f)}$ and let $\alpha$ be the <residue> class of $x$. Division with remainder represents every class by a <polynomial> of degree less than $n$, so every element is a <polynomial> in $\alpha$ and $f(\alpha)=0$. A nonzero class represented by $u$ has $\gcd(u,f)=1$ because $f$ is irreducible. Bézout's identity gives $au+bf=1$, making the class of $a$ its inverse. Thus $K$ is a <field>, containing $F$ through its constant classes.

A <splitting field> of a nonconstant <polynomial> is an extension in which it factors into linear factors and which is generated over the original <field> by its roots. To construct one, choose an irreducible factor and use the preceding quotient construction to adjoin a root. Divide off a linear factor in that extension and repeat on the remaining <polynomial>. Each stage lowers the remaining degree, so finitely many stages suffice. The <field> generated by all roots in the resulting <field> is the required <splitting field>. This induction works with repeated roots and does not assume separability. The two roman clauses in the source specify properties of this one construction; they are not separate questions.

= a
{parent=2004/B/B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2004/B/B2/a}

The roots are $\pm\sqrt{-5}$, so one root already supplies both. The <polynomial> has no rational root and is irreducible of degree two. Thus its <splitting field> is $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-5})$, with $\boxed{[K:\mathbb Q]=2}$.

= b
{parent=2004/B/B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2004/B/B2/b}

Put $\alpha=\sqrt[3]2$ and $\zeta=e^{2\pi i/3}$. The three roots are $\alpha,\alpha\zeta,\alpha\zeta^2$, so the <splitting field> is $K=\mathbb Q(\alpha,\zeta)$. <Eisenstein's criterion> at two gives $[\mathbb Q(\alpha):\mathbb Q]=3$. This intermediate <field> is real, while $\zeta$ is not real, and $\zeta$ satisfies $x^2+x+1=0$. Its degree over the intermediate <field> is therefore exactly two. By the tower law, $\boxed{[K:\mathbb Q]=6}$.

= c
{parent=2004/B/B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2004/B/B2/c}

Over $\mathbb F_5$,
$$
x^3+3=(x-3)(x^2+3x+4).
$$
The quadratic discriminant is $9-16\equiv3$, while the squares modulo five are $0,1,4$. Thus that factor is irreducible. Adjoining either quadratic root creates a degree-two extension and supplies the other root by the sum-of-roots relation. Consequently the <splitting field> is $\mathbb F_{25}$ and $\boxed{[K:\mathbb F_5]=2}$.

= C
